#: SettingsForm.pas:631
msgid "How the banks of a ROM bigger than 32K are switched. Auto-detect guesses it from the writes the code makes to the mapper registers; if a game does not start, choose another one. Changing it inserts the cartridge again"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:635
msgid "ROMs of up to 32K never need a mapper."
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:638
msgid "RAM mapper:"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:641
msgid "Auto (off when a tape starts the machine)"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:642
msgid "On (128K of mapper RAM)"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:643
msgid "Off (64K of RAM, as an MSX1)"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:644
msgid "The memory mapper answers at ports FCh-FFh. Some tape games (the ZX Spectrum conversions) write to port FEh for their own purposes and, with the mapper there, move the RAM under themselves; Auto leaves the mapper off when Auto load starts a tape. Takes effect with the next reset"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:648
msgid "MSX-DOS 2 needs the mapper."
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:649
msgid "MSX 1 compatibility mode"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:650
msgid "Some MSX1 programs use FFFFh as ordinary RAM or look for RAM in a primary slot alone, which the MSX2's expanded slot 3 breaks. With this on no slot is expanded (the RAM is slot 3 alone, the sub-ROM is in slot 2) and there is no disk drive. It is also in the Options menu. Resets the machine"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:657
msgid "On the MSX the cursor keys are always the cursor keys; with this on they also move joystick 1, and the right Ctrl is its first button"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:660
msgid "Commodore 64"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:664
msgid "Numeric keypad and right Ctrl as a joystick"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:665
msgid "The keypad's 8, 2, 4 and 6 (and the diagonals 7, 9, 1 and 3) are the joystick's directions, 0, 5 and the right Ctrl its fire button"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:667
msgid "Cursor keys and Ctrl as a joystick"
msgstr ""

#: SettingsForm.pas:668
msgid "The cursor keys are the joystick's directions and Ctrl (either) its fire button, and are not the C64's own cursor keys and CTRL (a key of the keyboard also reads as a joystick direction on the real machine). Scroll Lock switches it while running"
msgstr ""

